out to regenerate the original '43/43→HOSE' evidence file under a non-overwritable name (e.g. `probe_exchange_ref_PREFIX.json`), per coding_guidelines §8 discipline.", "Independently refetch 1-2 more VCI 1-minute-bar sessions (beyond 08-14) and reconcile against a live q.ref snapshot to extend the tick-level mechanism proof beyond a single day.", "Confirm no other code path besides `_limit_price`/`no_chase_ceiling.py` still reads the pre-fix vintage anchor definition before this is considered fully closed (FIX.md §5 already discloses ex-right-day sizing/qty as an open gap)."], "summary": "This finding holds up under adversarial review. I independently re-derived its two headline claims from primary sources rather than trusting the write-up: a fresh BigQuery query against tav2_bq.corporate_action confirmed SSI's [REDACTED] ex-right event (20% bonus + 1,000đ cash dividend) exactly as described, and a fresh independent pandas recompute from the cited vwap_daily.csv reproduced the N=1618 UPCOM anchor-deviation distribution (median 0.361%, p90 1.271%, 15.5% of sessions >1%, 1.2% >τ=3%) to full precision. The regulatory framework (HOSE/HNX=prior close, UPCOM=volume-weighted average of round-lot matched trades, ±7/10/15% bands) and the exchange-misclassification bug (Quote.exchange defaulting silently to 'HOSE' because DNSE's payload uses marketId, not exchange/market/floorcode) are both corroborated by the actual brokers.py code and its subsequent fix commit. The claim that BigQuery has no usable reference-price source was independently re-verified: Trading_Value is a derived Volume×Close column with zero real precision, and ticker.Price is unadjusted for corporate actions. The one real flaw is procedural, not substantive: the probe_exchange_ref.json artifact was overwritten by a later regression run before I could inspect the original pre-fix state, so that one sub-claim rests on git history rather than a live-reproducible file — worth fixing going forward but not a reas